# J16 — Headless / plain parity
# --plain and a non-TTY pipe of the same commands. Plain output must stay
# useful (no ANSI dependency, no interaction-only state) and honor the
# mode-parity contract in docs/assessments/mode-parity.md.
name: Headless and plain parity
cwd: @TMP_TARGET
env: HOME=@TMP_HOME
launch-raw: @KUZUSHI --plain; printf '\nEXIT:%s\n' $?
wait: 8
snap: bare-plain-summary
expect: SESSION SUMMARY
expect: EXIT:0
ask: Bare --plain — is the output a self-contained non-interactive summary with no prompt or interaction-only advice?
launch-raw: @KUZUSHI --plain --target @TMP_TARGET findings 2>&1 | head -40
wait: 8
snap: plain-findings
ask: --plain findings — line-oriented, no box drawing, no ANSI requirement, honest about zero findings on a fresh target?
launch-raw: @KUZUSHI --target @TMP_TARGET findings 2>&1 | cat | head -40
wait: 8
snap: nontty-pipe-findings
ask: Non-TTY pipe (no --plain) — did it auto-degrade to plain output identical in substance to --plain, or leak interactive chrome/escape codes?
launch-raw: @KUZUSHI demo 2>&1 | tail -30
wait-for: 120 finding
wait: 2
snap: nontty-demo
ask: Piped `kuzushi demo` — does the headless run narrate progress and findings usefully without the TUI (mode-parity: same value, plain surface)?
launch-raw: @KUZUSHI --plain --target @TMP_TARGET tools 2>&1 | head -30
wait: 8
snap: plain-tools
ask: --plain tools — is the catalog usable for scripting (stable names, one per line), with no hidden interaction-only state?
launch-raw: @KUZUSHI --plain scan; printf '\nEXIT:%s\n' $?
wait: 8
snap: implicit-scan-refused
expect: explicit target
expect-not: EXIT:0
launch-raw: @KUZUSHI --plain scan @TMP_TARGET; printf '\nEXIT:%s\n' $?
wait: 30
snap: explicit-scan-terminal
ask: Explicit scripted scan — did the process wait for terminal state, emit its run reference/bundle, and return a status-derived exit code?
